1
00:00:00,284 --> 00:00:02,206
The conversation is familiar.

2
00:00:04,251 --> 00:00:09,022
The destination isn't. ‘Please use our
updated payment details for

3
00:00:09,034 --> 00:00:09,909
this invoice.’

4
00:00:12,284 --> 00:00:13,526
Same supplier.

5
00:00:14,418 --> 00:00:15,620
Same retainer.

6
00:00:16,507 --> 00:00:17,994
One new recipient.

7
00:00:18,881 --> 00:00:23,136
The FBI advises verifying payment-detail
changes through an independent

8
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:23,670
channel.

9
00:00:24,571 --> 00:00:28,707
PayGuard Verify turns that advice into a
review: it detects the change,

10
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:32,249
holds approval, records an authorized
correction, and remembers

11
00:00:32,261 --> 00:00:33,788
what the next review should know.

12
00:00:34,284 --> 00:00:38,054
In the public demo, a synthetic Northstar
invoice arrives.

13
00:00:40,085 --> 00:00:44,018
PayGuard compares it with the established
recipient, flags the mismatch,

14
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:45,227
and holds approval.

15
00:00:47,268 --> 00:00:50,969
A human verifies through a known channel
and records the correction.

16
00:00:53,024 --> 00:00:55,312
No supplier payment is ever sent.

17
00:00:58,284 --> 00:00:59,567
Now the memory.

18
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:04,669
The approved policy is written to Walrus
Mainnet — thirty-two confirmed

19
00:01:04,681 --> 00:01:05,285
records.

20
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:07,941
Same later invoice.

21
00:01:09,097 --> 00:01:12,010
Memory off: the baseline hold still fires.

22
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,049
Memory on: genuine recall brings back the
rule we approved, with citations.

23
00:01:19,219 --> 00:01:23,097
This is a replay of a real SDK capture,
and labeled that way.

24
00:01:24,284 --> 00:01:28,380
A hostile instruction hidden in the
invoice doesn't skip verification.

25
00:01:34,284 --> 00:01:36,328
Memory isn't the only proof.

26
00:01:38,387 --> 00:01:43,103
PayGuard sells its analysis through x402:
the server quotes an HTTP

27
00:01:43,115 --> 00:01:47,238
402, a wallet approves exact bytes, and
the report is delivered.

28
00:01:49,279 --> 00:01:54,742
Three Mainnet payments of 0.001 SUI, each
verified by Tatum over Sui

29
00:01:54,754 --> 00:01:55,766
gRPC.

30
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:01,232
Synthetic invoices, and the payer and
merchant are two wallets

31
00:02:01,244 --> 00:02:02,051
I control.

32
00:02:04,079 --> 00:02:07,902
The report: hold, and reconcile the
conflicting recipient.

33
00:02:12,284 --> 00:02:14,912
Anyone can try it free, no wallet needed.

34
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:22,837
Connect a Sui wallet for a private
workspace.

35
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:31,531
Everything is open source, with a
published write-up.

36
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:36,722
PayGuard doesn't promise to stop fraud.

37
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:42,967
It makes the check explicit, remembers the
correction, and shows receipts

38
00:02:42,979 --> 00:02:43,760
you can open.

